# The Lock - a lock keeper, four gates, and a boat that comes down after hours.
# Written for the library. One person, one procedure, and the question of what a schedule is for.

title: The Lock
start: start

== start ==
bg river_jetty
music room_tone
The lock is one chamber and four gates and it takes eleven minutes to work a
boat through it, and you have done it eleven thousand times, which is a number you
worked out one winter and have not repeated to anybody.
The hours are on a board by the office: six to eight, twelve to two, five to seven.
Outside those, the gates stay shut, and the water stays where you left it, and that
is what a schedule is for.
-> the_evening

== the_evening ==
It is ten past seven. The last boat of the day went down at twenty to, a hire boat
with six people and a dog, and you have the paddles down and the balance beams
pulled shut and the office light off.
A boat comes up the cut with its light on. That is not a thing that happens.
-> the_boat

== the_boat ==
It is a narrowboat, seventy feet of it, and it is coming slowly and it is alone. The
man on the tiller lifts a hand and says the thing they all say.
boater: Evening. Am I too late?
The answer is yes. You have given that answer four times this year and every one of
them turned round without an argument.
-> the_choice

== the_choice ==
You are standing at the bottom gate with the windlass in your hand, which you have
not put down since seven, and the water in the chamber is at the level of the lower
cut, which is where it should be at this hour.
* Work him through. Eleven minutes. -> work_him
* Say no, the way you have said it four times. -> say_no
* Ask him why he is late. -> ask_him

== work_him ==
You open the top paddles and the water starts and the boat comes in and the man says
nothing at all, which is unusual, and the dog that is not there is noticeable because
every hire boat has one.
He is standing on the back deck with both hands on the tiller and he does not look at
the gates, or at the water, or at you. He looks at the office window, which is dark.
-> the_through

== say_no ==
you: You're too late. I'm shut.
boater: Right.
He does not argue. He puts the tiller over and the bow comes round in the wide part
and he goes back up the cut the way he came, slow, with the light on, and you stand
and watch him to the bend because that is what there is to do.
-> ending_refused

== ask_him ==
you: You're late.
boater: I know.
you: Where are you making for?
boater: Anywhere past the lock.
He says it plainly and then, because you have not said no yet, he says the rest.
boater: I was moored above the bend and there's a man been walking the towpath all
evening. He hasn't got a boat and he hasn't got a dog and he's stopped at mine twice.
-> the_man

== the_man ==
You look back up the way he came. The towpath is a pale line and there is nothing on
it that you can see, and you have worked this cut for nineteen years and you know that
"nothing you can see" at night is a different sentence from "nothing".
You have two things in your hand, which are a windlass and a telephone that works.
* Work him through and walk up the towpath yourself after. -> work_then_walk
* Ring the office and let somebody else decide. -> ring_office
* Work him through and say nothing to anybody. -> the_through

== work_then_walk ==
You work him through. Eleven minutes. He says thank you at the top gate and goes on
into the dark, and you put the paddles down and shut the gates behind him and take the
torch off its hook.
The towpath is two miles to the bend. You walk it as far as the bridge with the torch
on, and there is a coat over the rail that was not there at six, and nothing else, and
you leave the coat where it is because it is not yours.
-> ending_the_coat

== ring_office ==
bg closet
The office closes at five and the phone rings four times and then a man from the
regional number answers and asks you for the details in the order he has them on his
screen: the boat, the registration, the time, the location, the nature of the concern.
You give him all of it in that order, which takes six minutes, and he says somebody
will be sent out, which is a sentence with no time on it.
The boat has gone by the time you put the phone down. You never got the number.
-> ending_the_number

== the_through ==
You work him through, eleven minutes, the same as every boat, and he says thank you
and goes on into the dark towards the next lock, which is six miles and is manned by
somebody who will be shutting at eight.
You put the paddles down and you shut the gates and you check the balance beams twice,
which you have not done since the year the ice took the bottom gate.
-> ending_schedule

== ending_refused ==
You lock the office and walk to the car and the cut is flat and quiet and the light
that was coming up it is going back down it, and that is the schedule.
You get the boat's number anyway, from the board at the office in the morning, because
the registration was on the bow and you are good at reading bows. You do not do
anything with it. It is in the book, which is a thing that has a number in it now, and
which had nothing in it the day before.
-> END

== ending_the_coat ==
The police come to the lock on the Thursday and ask about the coat, and you tell them
the time you walked it and how far, and the man from the boat rings the office a week
later to say he got to the next lock and then to a mooring and that it was nothing, or
that he did not want to say, and you cannot tell which from his voice.
You have worked four more boats through after hours since. Nobody has asked you about
it. The board by the office still says five to seven, and you are the only person who
knows what you do with the eleventh hour.
-> END

== ending_the_number ==
The regional man rings back in the morning for the boat's number and you do not have
it, and he writes that down in the words he has for it, which are "insufficient
detail".
There is a folder with a line in it now that says this, and your name is on the line,
and the man on the towpath was never mentioned by anybody. You work the boats through
in the hours on the board and you put the windlass down at seven, and the cut is the
same as it was, and you have been doing this nineteen years.
-> END

== ending_schedule ==
You put the windlass in the office and you drive home with the radio on and it is
twenty to eight, which is the time you always get home on a Tuesday.
In the morning the water is where you left it and the gates are shut and there is
nothing on the towpath, and you look anyway, from the car, before you unlock.
The board by the office says six to eight, twelve to two, five to seven, and you wrote
it out in 2019 and it has never once been wrong.
-> END
